# The ignore file for `Containerfile.linux.mcp`, and it is the strongest
# statement this repository makes about what the mcp component is.
#
# BuildKit looks for `<dockerfile>.dockerignore` beside the Dockerfile before it
# looks for `.dockerignore` at the context root, so this scopes the rules to
# this one image and leaves the root alone -- which matters, because a test in
# `usage/tests/test_all.py` walks the repository root for container files by
# NAME and refuses them there.
#
# THE BOUNDARY IS STRUCTURAL, NOT A PROMISE.
#
#   The MCP surface is an HTTP CLIENT of the read API: a forwarded reader
#   token, no store access, no account outside its scope.  Handing it the
#   DuckDB engine or the store's query layer would be a privilege escalation
#   and would defeat the split.  The Containerfile could simply not COPY them
#   -- but then the next edit could, by accident, and nothing would say so.
#
#   With this file, `server/srv/store.py`, `duck.py`, `query.py`, `api.py` and
#   `serve.py` ARE NOT IN THE BUILD CONTEXT AT ALL.  A `COPY server/srv/` added
#   to the Containerfile fails the build by name rather than quietly producing
#   a fatter image with a database handle in it.  That is the same reason the
#   read API is gated by `readers.json` rather than by being unreachable: a
#   property enforced by the shape of the thing beats one enforced by everyone
#   remembering.
#
# The context is the REPOSITORY ROOT, because the image needs `LICENCE`,
# `server/srv/mcp.py` and `server/oci/entrypoint-mcp.sh`, and no one of them is
# a parent of the others.
#
# Last match wins, so the `__pycache__` line at the bottom is what keeps a
# developer's stale byte-code out of a published image.
*
!/LICENCE
!/server/srv/mcp.py
!/server/oci/entrypoint-mcp.sh
**/__pycache__
